    <!-- ================= ZONE B: x=50-58, TEXTURELESS solid surfaces ================= -->
    <!-- Canonical gate-should-fire case. wallB1 sits directly on the y=0
         flight path - this is the wall_approach recording's target.

         REPLACED (Step BG): was a 16m-wide, 6m-tall flat wall. Two
         independent HeavyStage normalization schemes (per-frame min-max,
         Step AY; rolling multi-frame window, Step BC) both failed to make
         this obstacle survivable in closed loop (Step AW, Step BF) -- a
         16m-wide surface fills most/all of several consecutive frames at
         close range, leaving no near/far depth contrast for an
         affine-invariant relative-depth model to anchor ANY normalization
         scheme against. That's a shared blind spot for BOTH stages (Step
         BF's own analysis), the same category narrowD1 (Zone D) was
         already built to avoid for exactly this reason (see its own
         comment below) -- Zone B just hadn't been given the same
         treatment yet.

         Sized from the camera's actual intrinsics (fx=205.4696, cx=320,
         width=640), not eyeballed: geometry.sector_index()'s 5 sectors
         are PIXEL-linear, not ANGLE-linear, so under the pinhole mapping
         theta=atan((x-cx)/fx) the centre sector is NOT 115/5=23deg as a
         naive equal-share estimate would give -- it's [-17.30, +17.30]
         deg, i.e. 34.6deg wide (~50% wider than the naive estimate),
         while the outermost sectors are compressed to 14.24deg each. A
         1.0m-wide box (half_y=0.5) stays purely within the centre
         sector's angular span down to d=1.6m (half-angle 17.35deg just
         exceeds 17.30deg there); at the task's specified 2m checkpoint
         its half-angle is 14.04deg, comfortably inside the centre
         sector's 17.30deg boundary with ~3.3deg of margin. It only
         starts spilling into sectors 1/3 below 1.6m, deepening further
         within the <0.5m close-range breakdown region Step AY/AZ already
         established applies to both stages independently -- i.e. the
         one range where this obstacle's own containment guarantee
         degrades is the SAME range where both stages' readings were
         already known to be unreliable for other reasons, not a new
         problem this replacement introduces.

         RESIZED AGAIN (Step BT/BU): the 1.0m x 1.5m box above made
         CheapStage fail as designed but never gave HeavyStage a
         signal with usable lead time either (Step BH: onset ~1.5m:
         Step BL/BM: every "pass" in closed loop was unavoidable
         start-position luck, not detection). Step BP found the real
         cause working backwards from measured geometry: heavy.py's
         80th-percentile aggregator needs an obstacle to occupy the top
         20% of its sector's PIXELS (not just its angular width) to
         reach the obstacle at all, and `sector_pixels` spans the FULL
         image column height regardless of the obstacle's own height --
         the 1.0m x 1.5m box was already wide enough (horiz_frac
         88-100% at 3-4m, N=11) but nowhere near tall enough
         (vert_frac only 16-21% at 3-4m), so height, not width, was the
         actual bottleneck. Sized this box from that constraint
         (min width 1.13m @ 4m, min height 1.87m @ 4m for area_frac to
         clear 20%, both computed from fx=205.4696/fy and the current
         11-sector default, Step BR) with real margin above both
         minimums: 1.2m wide x 2.5m tall gives area_frac 26.75%/4m,
         35.67%/3m, 53.51%/2m -- geometric prediction only at this
         point; Step BV verifies it against a real recording next.
         Single panel, not the "several
         boxes with gaps" option Step BU also offered -- the numbers
         above already leave the outer 9 of 11 sectors completely
         clear at every relevant range (this panel occupies at most
         ~2 of 11 sectors even at 2m), so the gaps-between-boxes
         design wasn't needed to preserve background contrast.

         REVERTED (Step BW): the Step BT/BU resize above was sized to
         work around the RELATIVE-depth checkpoint's percentile-based
         aggregation (needing the obstacle to occupy a large-enough
         PIXEL FRACTION of its sector for a percentile threshold to
         reach it at all). Step BW switches HeavyStage to a METRIC
         checkpoint instead, which reads depth in metres directly, no
         percentile aggregation over pixel fraction, no normalization
         step of any kind -- the entire premise the BT/BU resize was
         solving for no longer applies. Reverted back to the 1.0m x
         1.5m box (Step BG) so the metric-checkpoint verification tests
         against the object it's actually meant to handle, not a second
         confound stacked on top. If metric verification (Step BX)
         fails, the BT/BU resize's own reasoning remains valid and
         available to reapply -- not deleted, just not layered in here
         while a bigger, independent fix is being tested first. -->

    <model name="wallB1">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>55 0 3 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>0.4 1.0 1.5</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>0.4 1.0 1.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.7 0.7 0.7 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.7 0.7 0.7 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <model name="boxB1">
